== A Husband’s Patient Correcting of His Wife

=== §. 24. #emph[Of the titles which a husband gives to his wife.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 13. 14. How a husband’s mildness is shown.]

The #emph[mildness] of a husband named above must be shown in his

- #emph[Speech.]
- #emph[Conduct.]

For as far as #emph[reverence] extends in the duties of wives, so far must #emph[mildness] be extended in the duties of husbands.

Whether a husband’s speech is #emph[to his] wife before her face, or #emph[about her] #footnote[Of his speech about her behind her back, see Treatise 2, part 2, §. 36. The speech of husbands to their wives sweetened with mildness.] behind her back, it must be sweetened with mildness.

1\. As for his speech to her: 1. the #emph[titles] by which he calls her, 2. the #emph[instructions] which he gives her, 3. the #emph[commandments] which he lays on her, 4. the #emph[reproofs] with which he checks her, must all be mixed with #emph[mildness.]

Among other #emph[titles,] the most ordinary and usual title, “wife,” #footnote[1\. Mild and kind titles.] is a mild and kind title, and the least offensive of all. If a husband gives his wife any other #emph[title,] it must be one that shows kindness, familiarity, love and delight. Such are all the titles which Christ gives to the church, as “Spouse,” “love,” “Dove,” and the like. I do not deny that in the Song of Solomon, and in other places of Scripture, many titles are given and words used by Christ to the church which are not fitting for husbands to use to their wives, because they are metaphorical and hyperbolical. But yet in them all we may observe tokens of amiability, kindness and mildness, which is the end for which I have cited his example.

Contrary to these are such titles as, on the one side, set the wife #footnote[Contrary, unseemly titles.] in too high a place over her husband, as “Lady,” “mistress,” “Dame,” “mother,” etc. And on the other side, such as set her in too low a rank, as “woman,” “wench,” etc. And their Christian names shortened, as “Sal,” “Mal,” “Bess,” “Nan,” etc. And names of kindred, as “sister” and “Cousin.” And insulting names, as “slut,” “drab,” “quean.” And names more fitting for beasts than for wives, as “Cole,” “Browne,” “Muggle,” etc.

#emph[Objection.] These are titles of mildness, kindness and great familiarity. For husbands call their wives by these names, not when they are angry and displeased with them, but ordinarily and usually, even when they are best pleased.

#emph[Answer.] The mildness and familiarity required of a husband must be such as may stand with his authority and place of eminence (as some of those names do not), and with that close union which is between man and wife above all others (as others do not), and with Christian gravity and discretion (as others do not). Christians therefore must take care that they do not justify corrupt customs by their practice.

=== §. 25. #emph[Of a husband’s manner of instructing his wife.]

2\. To #emph[instruction] the apostle expressly joins #emph[meekness:] #footnote[Instruction to be given with meekness.] “Instruct with meekness” (he says) “those who oppose themselves.” If ministers must use #emph[meekness] when they instruct their people, much more must husbands when they instruct their wives. If #footnote[2 Tim 2:25 τοὺς ἀντιδιατιθεμένους.] meekness must not be laid aside in a case of #emph[opposition,] then it must be laid aside in no case and at no time.

To show meekness in this case, let these rules be observed. #footnote[Directions for instructing with meekness.]

1\. Note the understanding and capacity of your wife, and fit your instructions to them. If she is of limited capacity, give “precept on precept, line on line, a little here, a little there.” A little at a time, often given (that is, something every day), will grow in time to a great measure; and it will so grow that, together with #emph[knowledge] of the thing taught, #emph[love] for the person who teaches will increase.

2\. Instruct her in private, between yourself and her, so that her ignorance may not be spread abroad. Private actions passing between man and wife are tokens of much kindness and familiarity.

3\. In the family, instruct the children and servants when she is present in such a way that she may learn knowledge by it. There can be no meeker or gentler manner of instructing than to instruct one person by instructing another.

4\. Together with your precepts, mix sweet and pithy persuasions, #footnote[Contrary, harshness.] which are proofs of great love.

Contrary to this is a harsh and rough manner of instructing: when husbands try to thrust into their wives’ heads, as it were by force, deep mysteries which they are not able to grasp; and yet if the wives do not grasp them, the husbands will be angry with them, and in anger give them abusive words, and proclaim their ignorance before children, servants and strangers. This harshness is ordinarily so fruitless, and besides so exasperates a woman’s spirit, that I think a husband would do better to leave out the duty altogether than to do it in such a manner.

=== §. 26. #emph[Of a husband’s manner of commanding his wife anything.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 43, 51, 52. 63, 64. #emph[etc.] Commandments to be ordered with mildness.]

3\. The commandments which a husband gives to his wife, whether they are #emph[affirmative] (bidding her to do something) or #emph[negative] (forbidding her to do this or that), must all be seasoned with #emph[mildness.] To this end, regard must be had to the #emph[matter] and the #emph[manner] of his commandments.

With respect to the #emph[matter,] the things which he commands #footnote[What things he may command his wife.] his wife to do must be

+ Such as are truly lawful and honorable.
+ Such as she is persuaded are so.
+ Such as become her place.
+ Such as are of weight and importance.

And on the other hand, the things which he forbids must be

+ Such as are truly unlawful to be done. #footnote[What things he may forbid his wife.]
+ Such as he can clearly prove to her to be unlawful.
+ Such as are unbecoming her place.
+ Such as will have some evil and harmful effect if they are done.

1\. To command a thing unlawful, or to forbid a thing which ought to be done, is to set his own authority against God’s. In that case he brings his wife into this strait: she must reject either God’s commandment or his. How then can she think that her husband loves her, when he brings her into such snares and straits that she must either fall into the gulf of God’s displeasure or dash against the rock of offending her husband? Mildness is far from such commandments.

2\. The same may be said of such things as to a wife’s conscience #footnote[Such things as a wife has doubts about are not to be pressed on her.] seem sinful, if they are enjoined on her, or seem her bounden duty, if they are forbidden; especially if she has any ground for her conscience out of God’s word. The conscience is subject to God alone. If it is forced, it will be a fearful horror, and a very hell, in the person whose conscience is forced: “She who has doubts is condemned” if she does what #footnote[Rom 14:23.] she has doubts about.

1\. #emph[Objection.] In doubtful matters the commanding power #footnote[Of what doubtful things the commanding power of a governor may be a warrant.] of a governor is a sufficient warrant and ground to settle the conscience of those who are under authority.

#emph[Answer.] 1. In things purely doubtful, about which the party in subjection has no warrant out of God’s word one way or the other, it may be so. But when the conscience does not doubt and hang in suspense, but is persuaded on some ground taken from God’s word that what is commanded is unlawful, or that what is forbidden is a bounden duty, then to do the one, or to leave the other undone, is a sin to the party so persuaded. And this is the #emph[doubting] (of which the apostle speaks) that condemns a man. In this case, to urge a wife to do this, or not to do that, is to urge her to sin, which a mild spirit and a loving heart will not do.

2\. Though the husband’s command is a sufficient warrant to the wife, and she ought to yield if he peremptorily presses her to this or that, yet the love and mildness required of a husband should make him so tender toward her as to forgo something of his power. When he sees her conscience troubled about his command, he should relieve her conscience by forbearing to press what seems so burdensome to her. A husband may sin in pressing that too much on his wife which she, at his pressing, may yield to without sin.

=== §. 27. #emph[Of a husband’s wise conduct when his wife is erroneously scrupulous.]#footnote[Treatise 3, §. 66.]

2\. #emph[Objection.] What if a husband, from his own knowledge, observes that his wife is erroneously scrupulous, and that she misinterprets and misapplies the word of God which she makes the ground of her scruple?

#emph[Answer.] He must first labor to settle her conscience by plainly showing her error, which is a true and a great token of love. If, despite all that he can do in that way, she cannot be brought to yield to what he would have, then he must carefully observe these two things.

+ Whether her refusal to yield is obstinacy or weakness.
+ Whether it concerns a slight or a weighty matter.

By the reasons which she gives, and her manner of pressing #footnote[How a wife’s refusal to yield may be discerned to be obstinacy or weakness.] them, he may discern whether weakness or obstinacy makes her hold out against him. If the reason she rests on, taken from God’s word, is doubtful, and may seem to support her somewhat to one who does not have a good, sound judgment and a sharp, discerning understanding, it is to be presumed that there is more weakness than stubbornness in her. But if she can give no good reason, but only seizes on every appearance that seems in any way to lean toward her words, and peremptorily holds to her conclusion, and stiffly stands on her own resolution, though the emptiness of her pretexts is clearly exposed to her, so that she has nothing further to object; or if she gives no reason at all but her own thought, notion and will, and yet refuses to yield, surely obstinacy possesses her heart. In a case of obstinacy it is very advisable that a husband stand on his power to maintain his authority, and by the best wisdom he can (using only such means as are lawful) bring her to yield from her stubbornness to what he requires; especially if the matter is weighty: as in the case where a religious man has married a popish wife, and she will not be moved by any reason to stop going to Mass, or to yield to going to the preaching of the gospel. But if through #emph[weakness] she cannot be persuaded of the lawfulness of what her husband requires, and the matter required is of no great consequence, and the weakness of her conscience causes no great error, a husband ought to show his mildness so far as to forbear pressing her conscience.

=== §. 28. #emph[Of a husband’s forbearing to press things unbecoming a wife’s place.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 43, 44. #emph[etc.]]

3\. Things unbecoming the place of a wife are dishonorable to her. For a husband to urge his wife by strict charge to do them shows more harshness than mildness. Had the spirit of that proud monarch Ahasuerus been milder toward his wife, he would not have pressed his wife so far to so unseemly a thing as he did, namely, to come before all his princes and people to make a show of her beauty. It is true indeed (as we showed #footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 28.] before) that she did wrong in refusing to yield to it when he peremptorily required it. But that offense on her part does not justify his deed and free him from all blame. It is noted that he was “merry with wine” when he gave that commandment, #footnote[Esth 1:10.] by which it is implied that his practice was more fitting for a drunken man than a sober one. Such is the practice of those who demand of their wives that they do such tasks as become maidservants rather than wives, or harlots rather than chaste women; such as going to taverns, alehouses, playhouses and such places where loose companions are.

=== §. 29. #emph[Of a husband’s pressing his authority in weighty matters.]

4\. To use a man’s authority about weighty matters, matters of importance, gives it such weight that it will be much better regarded. For in this way a wife will either be brought to yield to what is commanded, or to condemn herself for not yielding. Yes, in this way a wife may see that it is not so much his own will that makes him use his authority in commanding, as the necessity of the thing itself, which tends especially to the good of her who does it. For the performance of a duty is for the most part most advantageous to the one who performs it, so that by this a husband shows love to his wife in pressing what he presses.

So that this token of love may appear more clearly, it is needful #footnote[Reasons for pressing a commandment are to be given.] that a husband add just and weighty reasons to his commandment, so that by them his wife may better discern the fitness, lawfulness, expediency and necessity of the things commanded. We know that all the things which God commands are weighty and necessary. Yes, his #emph[will] (being the very rule and ground of all goodness) makes things absolutely necessary; yet to his commandments he is accustomed to add weighty reasons, showing on the one side the benefit and blessedness that will come to those who obey his commandments, and on the other side the harm and misery that will fall on the heads of those who refuse to obey. By this he shows the great and good regard which he bears toward us, and the earnest desire he has for our good. So a husband may show much love and kindness even in his commandments.

=== §. 30. #emph[Of husbands’ too great pride in commanding.]

Contrary to this is the peremptory pride of husbands, when they will have their own will done. It makes no difference whether the thing commanded is lawful or unlawful, whether their wives’ consciences can yield to it or not, whether it is consistent with the honor of their places or not, and whether it is weighty or light: it is their will that it should be done, and done it shall be; that is all the reason they will give. Some think it a glory to command whatever they please, and think that there is no proof of their authority, and of their wives’ subjection, except in such things as they command by their own will, without any further ground or reason. If such husbands meet with defiance, if, though they command much, they do not find a corresponding performance, they may thank themselves, for they take the ready course to have their authority despised and even trodden underfoot.

=== §. 31. #emph[Of husbands’ rare and mild use of their commanding power.]

Husbands must have regard to the #emph[manner] of using their authority in commanding, as well as to the #emph[matter.]

With respect to the #emph[manner,] his commandments must be

+ #emph[Rare,] not too frequent.
+ By way of #emph[entreating,] not too peremptory.

Authority is like a sword, which with overmuch use will #footnote[1\. Commanding must be rare.] be blunted, and so fail to do the service which it otherwise might when there is most need. A wise, grave, peaceable man may always have his sword in readiness, and that also very bright, keen and sharp; but he will not be very ready to pluck it out of its scabbard. He rather keeps it for a time of need, when it will be of most use to him. So husbands who are too frequent in their commands show themselves neither grave, nor wise, nor lovers of peace.

As the use of a husband’s authority in commanding must be #footnote[2\. Commanding must be by way of entreating. Phlm 9.] rare, so when there is occasion to use it, it must be tempered with such mildness and moderation that (following Saint Paul’s example), though he has power to command what is fitting, yet he would rather entreat it “on the basis of love.” Note how mildly Abram frames his words to his wife: “Please say” #footnote[Gen 12:13.] (he says) “you are my sister.” Though the thing he required smacked of too much weakness, yet his manner of requiring it well became a kind husband.

=== §. 32. #emph[Of husbands’ insolence and peremptoriness.]

Contrary to this is the insolence of many, who cannot speak to their wives except by way of command. Their authority is like a swaggerer’s sword, which cannot rest long in the sheath, but is drawn out on every small occasion. This frequent use of commanding makes their commandments disregarded. The same may be said of those who are too peremptory in commanding. There must be no saying no to what they say; they will have their mind done by command, and no other way. No persuasion, no entreaty may be used; they would rather not have their will done at all than have it done other than by absolute command. Indeed, they will not allow others, in case of any refusal, to entreat or persuade, but will try what they can do absolutely by authority. So, just as steel often breaks when one tries how far it will bend, so by putting their authority to the utmost trial they often lose all their authority. In that case “the remedy” (as we say) “is in their own hands.”

=== §. 33. #emph[Of a husband’s reproving his wife.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 47. Husbands ought to reprove.]

4\. The authority and charge which God has given a husband over his wife require that, as good and just occasion is offered, he should reprove her. For this is a special means to draw her from those sins in which she might otherwise live and lie, yes, and die also; and so live, lie and die under God’s wrath. To free a wife from that misery and wretchedness is as great a token of love as to pull her out of the water when she is in danger of drowning, or out of the fire when she is in danger of burning. Solomon calls reproofs “life-giving,”#footnote[Prov 15:31.] and expressly notes that “reproofs”#footnote[Prov 6:23.] are “the way to life”: a means to breed and preserve spiritual life, and to bring one to eternal life, and so to escape death and damnation. In these respects rebukes are called “a precious balm,” or “excellent oil,” which may heal a wound but makes none: it “does not break the head,”#footnote[Ps 141:5. See the fruits of reproof, Treatise 3, §. 47.] as the psalmist says. On this ground, no doubt, it is noted of many good husbands, who were beyond all question loving, kind, meek and mild husbands, that they reproved their wives: as Jacob,#footnote[Gen 30:2.] Job,#footnote[Job 2:10.] David,#footnote[2 Sam 6:21, 22.] and others.

=== §. 34. #emph[Of neglecting reproof.]

Contrary to this is the servile and timid spirit of many husbands, who are reluctant to offend and (as they think) to provoke their wives, and so choose rather to let them continue in sin than to tell them of it. In this they both dishonor their place, and the image of God which they bear by virtue of their place, and also in effect and in truth hate their wives. The law implies this where it says, “You must not hate your brother in your heart, #footnote[Lev 19:17.] but you must directly rebuke your neighbor, and not allow sin to rest on him.”

=== §. 35. #emph[Of ordering reproof well in its matter.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 47. A husband’s reproof must be sweetened with mildness.]

That a husband may clearly demonstrate that his reproving of his wife is truly a fruit of his love, he must take special care to sweeten it, especially with mildness: for it is the bitterest pill that a husband can give to a wife. It is a verbal correction, and in that respect a middle means (if I may so speak) between admonition and correction, partaking somewhat of both. It goes no further than words, and so is an admonition; the words of a reproof are sharp, and so it is a correction. Though it is only a mild correction, yet it is a sharp admonition, and all the correction which a husband can give his wife by himself: for we will #footnote[§. 44.] show later that he must not proceed to blows and strokes.

To sweeten #emph[reproof with mildness,] regard must be had (as was noted before about #emph[commanding)] both to its #emph[matter] and to its #emph[manner.]

The #emph[matter] of reproof must be #footnote[Matter of reproof. 1. Just.]

+ #emph[Just.]
+ #emph[Weighty.]

Justice requires that what he reproves his wife for be a #emph[truth,] and a #emph[known truth,] indeed a thing of which he is certain. Christ, in giving direction for reproving rightly, lays this down as a ground: “If your brother trespasses, etc.”#footnote[Matt 18:15.] A trespass therefore must go before reproof: where there is no trespass, reproof is unjust.

Again, the apostle advises that an accusation should not #footnote[1 Tim 5:19.] be received “except on the testimony of two or three witnesses.” By this he implies that an idle report must not be received; rather, where blame is laid, there must be two or three witnesses to confirm it, so that he who censures may have good and sure ground for what he does. Indeed, that advice was given in particular about an elder, but from the less to the greater it will follow that it is good advice concerning wives: for no kind of person must be more wary in laying blame on another, and reproving them for it, than a husband with his wife.

Equity further requires that the #emph[matter] for which a husband #footnote[2\. Weighty.] reproves his wife be #emph[weighty:] namely, some fault that is dangerous to her soul, harmful to their worldly condition, or contagious through its bad example to children and others in the family; but most of all, sin against God, which provokes his wrath and pulls down his heavy curse on him, her and the whole family.

When what a wife is reproved for is a truth, a known truth and a weighty truth, the husband in performing this duty justifies his deed and shows that there was need of it, and so gives evidence of his love. He makes his reproof pierce more deeply, and so makes her more ashamed of her fault. From this it will follow that she will either amend her fault or at least have her mouth stopped, so that she will have nothing to object against it. The reproofs of the three saints mentioned before, Jacob,#footnote[Gen 30:2.] Job#footnote[Job 2:10.] and David,#footnote[2 Sam 6:21, 22.] corresponded to these points of justice and wisdom; and their effects corresponded to those which we have noted in this reason, as the silence of the three wives implies: for none of them answered back.

=== §. 36. #emph[Of undue reproof.]

Contrary to the justice and equity named above are overready #footnote[Contrary, 1. Credulity.] #emph[credulity] and undue #emph[suspicion. Credulity] is when belief is given to every idle report, and blame is then laid on the wife before any just proof is made of what she is blamed for. By this it often comes to pass that she is wrongfully and unjustly blamed; and if she is, what good fruit can come from such reproofs? Indeed, what evil fruits are not likely to come from them, such as secret discontent (if not malice and hatred) and open contentions and quarrels?

The same may be said of light and causeless #emph[suspicion,] which #footnote[2\. Suspicion.] is the mother of jealousy and the very bane of marriage. The devil takes great advantage from it against them both, seeking by it to untie the knot which God has so firmly tied between them. #emph[Suspicion] is to the mind what a colored glass is to the eye: it shows things to the sight not as they truly are in their own true color, but as the color of the glass is. #emph[Suspicion] will make a man twist everything that his wife does, and blame her many times for things that are praiseworthy. In that case, what can be thought but that a husband is seeking an advantage against his wife, rather than any good for her?

If to those two vices named above #emph[(credulity] and #emph[suspicion)] he #footnote[3\. Rashness.] adds #emph[rashness] and hastiness in reproving, and makes every small and light matter which he in any way dislikes a matter for reproof, does he not proclaim to all who come to know it that he loves scolding more than he loves his wife? Indeed, is this not the ready way to make all his reproofs (if not scorned) lightly regarded? What then will be the profit of them?

=== §. 37. #emph[Whether a husband may reprove his wife for such things as he is guilty of.]

To the #emph[matter] of reproof some add that a husband ought not to reprove his wife for a fault of which he himself is guilty. But I doubt this direction. I do not deny that he ought to take special care that he is not guilty of the crime for which he blames his wife. Otherwise, 1. He blunts the edge of his reproof, so that it cannot readily pierce into her heart. 2. He makes it rebound back on himself with these reproaches: “Physician, heal #footnote[Luke 4:23. Matt 7:5. Rom 2:21. Rom 2:1.] yourself!” “You hypocrite! First take the beam out of your own eye.” “You who teach others, do you not teach yourself?” 3. He is a heavy witness against himself; for in judging another he condemns himself. But to infer from this that, because he is guilty of the same vices as are in his wife, he ought not to reprove her, though she deserves to be reproved, is hardly sound and good divinity. For in this way he makes himself guilty of a double fault: #emph[one,] of committing the sin himself; #emph[the other,] of allowing his wife to lie in it. Whereas if he reproved his wife, he might by it reclaim both her and himself: for I do not doubt that his reproving of his wife would strike deeper into his own conscience than if a third person reproved them both. How Judah and David#footnote[Gen 38:26. 2 Sam 12:13.] were struck to the heart after they had given sentence against the very crimes of which they themselves were guilty! It is good advice that no man be guilty of what he reproves in his wife; but it is no good rule to say that no man ought to reprove his wife for what he is guilty of.

=== §. 38. #emph[Of ordering reproof well in its manner.]

Directions like those which were given for the manner #footnote[Manner of reproving.] of commanding must be observed in the #emph[manner] of reproving.

Reproofs therefore must be

- #emph[Rare.]
- #emph[Meek.]

When reproofs are #emph[seldom] used, and only on urgent and #footnote[1\. Seldom.] necessary occasion, 1. It shows that a husband takes no delight in rebuking his wife, but is even forced to it. 2. It makes his wife regard it much more. 3. It is likely to work a more perfect cure, for seldom and rare reproofs commonly pierce most deeply.

Contrary to this is continual scolding and finding fault with a #footnote[Contrary, continual scolding.] wife for everything amiss. If not only the wife herself, but a child, or a servant, or anyone else in the house does wrong, the wife will be blamed for it. This is too common a fault in husbands, by which they greatly provoke their wives; yes, and many times make them regard a reproof no more than any other word. For as birds which always live in belfries where there is much ringing are not frightened at all by the loud sound, so wives who have their ears filled from time to time with their husbands’ rebukes are brought by habit not to be moved by them at all.

2\. That a reproof must be given in #emph[meekness] is clear from #footnote[2\. Reprove mildly. Gal 6:1.] the apostle’s general precept to restore one “with a spirit of meekness”: for a right manner of reproving is particularly intended by it. Now of all with whom we have to do, there is no fitter object for meekness than a wife, who in a more special manner than any other is “your own flesh.”

#emph[Meekness] concerns both #emph[secrecy] of place and #emph[softness] of words.

When a husband is alone with his wife, then is the fittest #footnote[3\. Reprove in secret. Matt 18:15.] time for reproof. In this way reproof will correspond to Christ’s direction: “tell him his fault between you and him alone” (Christ says this of a brother); but no brother must be treated more tenderly than a wife. In this way also it will soak better into her soul, when no thought of dishonor and discredit arises to hinder its work; such thoughts will readily arise when a reproof is given in public before others. In this way, likewise, occasion to despise her will be taken away from children and servants, which they would otherwise quickly take if she were rebuked before them, concluding from it that she is kept under as much as they are. Now because she is a joint governor of them with him, he ought by all means to maintain her reputation before them.

1\. #emph[Question.] What if she does not regard a rebuke in secret? #footnote[Direction for reproving a wife who does not regard a secret reproof. Matt 18:15.]

#emph[Answer.] He may follow Christ’s direction, “take one or two others along”: namely, wise, grave, faithful friends, if possible of her own kindred, such as her parents (if she has any living), or those who are next to parents in the course of nature (if they are not partial on her side); and he may rebuke her before them, but by no means before any of the household under her government.

2\. #emph[Question.] What if her fault is public, one that #footnote[Direction for reproving a wife whose offense is public.] may be a bad example to those of the household, since it was committed in their sight or brought to their knowledge some other way?

#emph[Answer.] He must wisely show his dislike of her fault in such a way that he in no way impairs her honor. He may therefore declare that such a thing was not well done, and warn his household beforehand against committing the like; indeed, sharply threaten them that if any of them does the like they will dearly regret it. And if any who are under correction offend in it, he may correct them all the more surely and severely, for the very reason that they have followed her example. In this way he will show great and good respect for his wife, and also a thorough dislike and hatred of her sin.

2\. “A soft tongue” (as Solomon notes) “breaks a bone,” #footnote[4\. Reprove gently. Prov 25:15.] that is, softens a hard heart and beats down a stubborn pride. How then will it work on a soft heart and a gentle disposition? If therefore a husband looks to do good by reproving his wife, his reproof must be so ordered that it may seem rather a gentle admonition than a sharp rebuke. He may and ought to declare her fault to her plainly, but in mild and meek terms, without reviling, insulting and degrading words.

#emph[Question.] What if her fault is a heinous, notorious sin?

#emph[Answer.] In an extraordinary case some sharpness may be #footnote[A reproof may be sharp, not bitter. Gen 30:2. Job 2:10.] used, as the reproofs of Jacob, Job and David show, for every one of them was sharp. But yet this sharpness must not be made bitter by any abusive language. A woman’s wickedness must not move a husband to be perverse and #footnote[2 Sam 6:21, 22. Rebuke never to be given by a man in anger.] violent, but rather to be all the more watchful over himself, that he keeps himself within the bounds of discretion and moderation. To this end it is fitting that husbands lay it down as a rule never to rebuke their wives when they are angry. Anger raises a dark mist before the eyes of reason, which, while it remains, keeps reason from giving any good direction. Indeed, anger is like a fire, and it so inflames a man and unsettles him that in his disorder he can keep no moderation or measure. However unable a man may be to rule himself when anger is stirred up, yet if beforehand, while “his eye is good,#footnote[Matt 6:22.] and his whole body full of light,” while he is “in tune” (as we say) and well tempered, he resolutely determines with himself not to do this or that thing in his anger, that earlier resolution will be a special means to make him forbear doing in anger what, if he did it, he could not well order and moderate in anger. For once he begins to do a thing in anger, the least provocation possible will be like bellows to blow that fire up into a flame.

Because of the violence of anger (in which women, because #footnote[Rebuke to be put off while a wife is angry.] of the weakness of their judgment, are for the most part most violent), it is also the part of a wise man to forbear this duty of reproving his wife even while she is angry. For as it is needful that he should be in a fit state to give a reproof, so it is just as needful that she should be in a fit state to take a rebuke. Anger both fills and festers one’s heart. The heart, then, being full of anger, what room is left for good advice? Will a man pour wine into a vessel full of water, or wait till all the water is drained out?

The heart also being so festered that it tastes of nothing but anger, what good can good advice do then?

It is therefore a special point of wisdom, and shows the good regard that a man bears for his wife, indeed it savors of much meekness and moderation, for a husband to weigh well both his own and his wife’s state of mind when he reproves her, and to forbear doing it while either he or she is angry.

=== §. 39. #emph[Of reproving a wife indiscreetly.]

Contrary to this is the indiscretion of husbands who regard neither place, nor persons, nor time, nor the state of mind of themselves or their wives, nor any other circumstance in reproving. Like Saul (who, at a table where there was a great feast, in the presence of his nobles and captains, when he was enraged with anger, not only rebuked but also reviled his son with most virulent and bitter words, and with such words that he did not spare his own wife; for in his rage he called his son “son #footnote[1 Sam 20:30.] of a perverse and rebellious woman”), like this foolish and furious Saul, I say, they take the most open place in the family, before children, servants and the whole house, to reprove their wives; and that with such bitter and disgraceful terms that either they provoke their wives to answer back to maintain (as they think) their own credit and reputation (so Jonathan #footnote[1 Sam 20:32.] was provoked to answer his father back), or else they give those of the house who see her trampled underfoot like this an occasion to set their feet on her too.

Most husbands are eager enough to reprove, but few do it with meekness and moderation. They cannot do it except in company, nor without bitter words. Many, in rebuking their wives, do not hesitate to use all the evil names they can think of, even such as tend not only to their wives’ dishonor but also to their own and their children’s disgrace. The reason for this is that they never rebuke except when they are angry, and so hardly know what they are doing. By this they also stir up anger in their wives, and yet for all that they do not hold back one bit more, but rather grow more violent: as when the heat of two fires meets together, the flame must be all the greater. Since this is the wrongheaded practice of many husbands, is it any wonder that ordinarily so little good and so much harm is done by reproving? Indeed, would it not be a wonder if any good and no harm were done by it? Therefore, though this is a duty, yet it is a duty to be used rarely and with great moderation.

So much for a husband’s mildness in his speech to his wife.
